WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.580
A limp stalk of celery stands in water for a few hours, and it can turn crisp again.

2
00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:12.840
You drink water every day and stay just as bendy.

3
00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:15.680
What is different about the plant's cells?

4
00:00:16.783 --> 00:00:18.543
Start with one of your own cells.

5
00:00:19.863 --> 00:00:21.503
A nucleus holds its instructions.

6
00:00:22.883 --> 00:00:27.063
A thin cell membrane wraps the cell and controls what goes in and out.

7
00:00:28.083 --> 00:00:30.843
Jelly-like cytoplasm fills the inside.

8
00:00:32.123 --> 00:00:36.843
A folded network, the endoplasmic reticulum, makes and moves materials.

9
00:00:38.403 --> 00:00:40.503
Mitochondria release energy from sugar.

10
00:00:41.783 --> 00:00:43.963
Small sacs called vacuoles store water.

11
00:00:45.017 --> 00:00:47.317
A celery cell has this whole kit too.

12
00:00:48.717 --> 00:00:52.797
Now we turn your cell into a celery cell, by adding one part at a time.

13
00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:56.890
First, a stiff cell wall closes around the outside.

14
00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:00.390
The membrane stays, just inside it.

15
00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:03.510
Think of the wall as a cardboard box.

16
00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:06.190
Second, chloroplasts.

17
00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:11.590
In green cells, these food makers capture energy from light to make sugar.

18
00:01:13.083 --> 00:01:15.503
Third, one large central vacuole.

19
00:01:16.463 --> 00:01:20.663
It is a water tank, and in a full-grown cell it fills most of the space.

20
00:01:21.767 --> 00:01:23.387
Now let water flow in.

21
00:01:24.547 --> 00:01:30.607
The tank swells. It pushes the cytoplasm and the membrane outward, until the

22
00:01:30.607 --> 00:01:32.807
membrane presses into the corners of the box.

23
00:01:33.917 --> 00:01:35.617
The stiff wall pushes back.

24
00:01:36.837 --> 00:01:39.937
It holds the rising pressure in, and the cell turns firm.

25
00:01:41.457 --> 00:01:44.837
Picture a water balloon filled tight inside a cardboard box.

26
00:01:45.817 --> 00:01:49.737
A celery stalk holds many of these firm cells, side by side.

27
00:01:50.997 --> 00:01:52.497
Together they hold the stalk up.

28
00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:57.170
Now the stalk sits on the counter all day, and it loses water.

29
00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:00.710
What happens to the tank, and then to the stalk?

30
00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.380
The tank shrinks, so it pushes less.

31
00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:12.300
The box is no longer pushed tight.

32
00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:16.140
Each cell goes soft, and the stalk goes limp.

33
00:02:17.183 --> 00:02:19.083
Put the limp stalk back in water.

34
00:02:20.483 --> 00:02:24.703
Water moves into the cells, the tanks fill again, and the stalk can turn crisp.

35
00:02:26.417 --> 00:02:28.457
So why does water not stiffen you?

36
00:02:29.597 --> 00:02:31.957
Your cells have the shared kit, but no box.

37
00:02:33.277 --> 00:02:36.937
With no stiff wall to push against, water cannot make them firm.

38
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.300
Your body is held up a different way.

39
00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.200
A skeleton of bones does that job.

40
00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:48.120
Your body also controls how much water stays inside you.

41
00:02:49.083 --> 00:02:53.763
Same shared kit inside, plus a box, a food maker and a water tank.

42
00:02:55.043 --> 00:02:59.623
When the tank is full, it pushes the cell out against the stiff box, and a soft

43
00:02:59.623 --> 00:03:00.583
plant stands up.
